Let async shader compilation skip small draws too (core option)

With async_shader_compilation on, RasterizerVulkan::AccelerateDrawBatchInternal
still makes a draw WAIT for its pipeline when it has six vertices or fewer
(wait_built = !async_shaders || num_vertices <= 6), most likely so that
screen-wide quads are never missing. On MoltenVK that wait is a whole
SPIR-V to MSL to Metal compile on the emulation thread.

On an iPhone 14 Pro (Majora's Mask 3D, async compilation on, patch 0005 on,
2026-10-19), the game ran at 60 fps with 6 ms of work per frame, and stopped
for 0.3 to 1.1 seconds at a new scene, each stop right after a Metal compile.
On the host (Tools/3ds-bench, the bridge's options, 25 s of play, the probe
since removed), every draw that still waited for its pipeline was one of
these: 53 waits, all of 3, 4 or 6 vertices.

This adds the option `citra_async_small_draws` (default disabled, so a frontend
that does not set it sees no change). When it is on, small draws are skipped
until their pipeline is built, as larger ones already are. The cost is a small
object or a screen-wide pass missing for a frame or two the first time it is
drawn. With the option off, the code path is the core's own; host runs fall in
the same run-to-run spread as without the patch (0 to 4 frames of 1800 differ
between runs of one build). With it on, a state saved and loaded back on the
host replays 300 frames with the sound identical and the picture different only
in the first frame, as before.

